notes:
Maintainer risk: HIGH — multiple silent-failure paths (query_subtree, fetchParents, query depth expansion) hide backend errors from clients; FileNode/copy handler is missing onExists/compareCaseInsensitively/onDestroyRemoveChildren entirely despite being documented as implemented; duplicate test-double infrastructure (test_support::MockBackend) diverges from canonical jmap-mail-server.
Confidence: 30% chance of missed bug. Least confident flags: JMAP-510h.12 (multi-level destroy partial-tree corner case) — needs an empirical test to confirm whether the current per-level all_covered check is observably wrong or just suboptimal; JMAP-510h.13 (PatchObject pointer-form keys) — depends on jmap-types PatchObject deserializer behavior I did not read in detail. Uncertainty driven by: (1) Did not deeply read jmap-server::handlers::handle_get/handle_query/handle_set to confirm they pass-through unrecognized args correctly (the cookie-cutter assumption is they do, but the assumption is not verified). (2) Did not check whether jmap-mail-server canonical sibling has the same query_subtree silent-error bug (if it does, JMAP-510h.4 propagation needs to go through the canonical first). (3) Did not test concurrent execution of /set against MemoryBackend — its Arc<Mutex> serialises all access so race detection is moot, but a real backend may have race exposure that the handlers do not consider. Would raise confidence if: I could run a fuzzer over the /set handler with random JSON inputs to catch panic paths and assertion violations, and could exercise the dispatcher with a backend that randomly returns Err at every public method to confirm error propagation.
API contract risk: MEDIUM — three trait-shape gaps with real production impact (FileNodeBackend::blob_exists returns bool not Result so backend connectivity errors silently become 'blob not found'; find_sibling_by_name case-folding algorithm is unspecified, creating cross-filesystem interop hazards; FileNodeBackend trait doc is silent on how principal_id maps to shareWith ACLs, inviting a backend implementation that follows the documented contract literally and silently permits cross-principal mutations); one security-contract violation in the reference impl (MemoryError::Display interpolates raw inner string, can leak parse error data containing user content); plus 12 hygiene/contract gaps (non_exhaustive missing on public types, duplicate re-exports binding upstream major version, undocumented handler arg shapes, query_subtree depth=0 doc/impl contradiction, with_account silent overwrite, seed_node accepts dangling parents, ChangeType divergence from canonical sibling). Most concerns concentrate in the trait contract and the reference impl, not the wire protocol; consumers using their own backend can work around several. Pre-1.0 status mitigates the versioning concerns somewhat but not the trait-shape ones.
API contract confidence: 30% chance of missed issue. Least confident flags: JMAP-510h.33 (case-folding interop hazard) — depends on whether real-world FileNode backends will actually be expected to interop across platforms with different case-folding semantics; if every deployment is single-platform, the concern is theoretical; JMAP-510h.45 (query_subtree depth=0) — the impl/doc contradiction is real but the handler short-circuits before reaching it, so the contract gap only matters for non-handler consumers of the trait method, which may not exist; JMAP-510h.34 + .43 (supports_type unused) — depends on workspace policy about whether the gate is intended for future capability extension vs. dead method to remove. Uncertainty driven by: (1) did not run cargo-public-api or rustdoc to enumerate the COMPLETE public surface of the crate, may have missed re-export chains through type sub-modules; (2) did not test what happens when a downstream consumer calls FileNodeBackend methods directly without going through register_filenode_handlers — the seam contracts may differ; (3) did not check whether the canonical sibling jmap-mail-server has had its own /review-library-supplement pass that filed parallel findings — if not, several of my 'propagation note' references are misleading because canonical has no plan-of-record; (4) did not verify whether the FileNode draft (draft-ietf-jmap-filenode-13) itself specifies case-folding semantics for compareCaseInsensitively — finding .33 may overstate the workspace's ownership of the decision.
Idiom rating: ACCEPTABLE — code works and follows most Rust conventions, but 8 idiom divergences from canonical sibling and from established Rust idioms (3 ownership, 2 error-handling, 2 type-design, 1 clippy). None are soundness-level; all are fixable as in-place refactors except .54 (case_insensitive: bool → enum), which is a trait-shape change.
Rust idiom confidence: 30% chance of missed issue. Least confident flags: JMAP-510h.50 (OnExists derive set — marked 'opinion' because the immediate code works; the move-out-of-shared analysis around 'match on_exists' inside a for-loop relies on a unit-variant non-destructive discriminant read that I am not 100% confident the compiler implements, vs. an explicit Copy derive that makes it unambiguous); JMAP-510h.51 (reconstituted dance — could be tackled by either fixing extract_account_id upstream or by not using the generic handler; the right fix is a workspace decision, not a filenode-only change). Uncertainty driven by: (1) I did not run 'cargo clippy --all-features -- -W clippy::pedantic -W clippy::nursery' which would surface specific lints like clippy::or_fun_call, clippy::manual_let_else, clippy::manual_while_let_loop that may have flagged additional sites I did not catch by reading. (2) I deliberately did not file 3 patterns visible in this crate's diff that are also present in canonical jmap-mail-server, because per the canonical-template rule those need a canonical-first fix: 'since_state.as_ref().parse().unwrap_or(0)' silent fallback (memory.rs:294 here; mail-server memory.rs:198 same shape), '.expect("derive(Serialize) on plain data is infallible")' (3 sites here; 17 in mail-server; centralized helper would help both), and the sentinel-bool pattern in the OnExists::Rename loop (filenode.rs:309-356; mail-server has no analog but the pattern is workspace-coherent). If canonical mail-server gets its own /review-rusty pass that flags any of these, I would file the filenode propagation finding then. (3) I did not study the jmap-server::handlers::handle_get/query/changes signatures to determine whether option (a)/(b)/(c) in finding .51 is least invasive — that requires reading jmap-server source which is out of scope here. Would raise confidence if: I could run cargo clippy with extended lints and a diff-aware filter to see precisely which clippy-level idioms the lint config catches today vs. which would catch additional sites.
API contract supplement (second pass): filed 6 new findings (.56-.61) clustering in two groups. (1) RFC 8620 §5.4 standard Foo/copy compliance gaps: .56 (onSuccessDestroyOriginal + destroyFromIfInState), .57 (ifFromInState), .58 (fromAccountNotFound vs accountNotFound). (2) Reference-impl + backend-contract polish: .59 (no in-account-copy cycle check), .60 (create_object docstring silent on atomic re-validation requirement), .61 (MemoryBackend ID counter collides with seed_node 'node-N' format).
API contract supplement confidence: 30% chance of further missed issue. The prior pass at .31-.46 was thorough on the FileNodeBackend trait surface (signatures, return types, doc gaps, re-exports) and on the MemoryBackend public type surface (non_exhaustive, error type, change log). This supplement found gaps in a different axis: spec-level Foo/copy wire-protocol args (.56-.58), which were not on the prior pass's lens because the prior pass focused on Rust API contract rather than JMAP wire contract. The remaining 30% covers: (a) jmap-server-side helpers (extract_account_id, server_fail_from_backend, handle_get etc.) that filenode-server delegates to but whose contract gaps would surface in this crate's consumers; (b) test-only paths in MockBackend that may have public-surface leakage I did not exhaustively audit; (c) FileNodeBackend default-impl interactions with custom backend overrides that I did not enumerate combinatorially. The strongest signal that the pass converged: every additional finding I considered traced back to either an existing prior-pass bead or to the consumer's responsibility (transport, persistence, auth) per the workspace kit-posture. Bikeshedding signal is reached. Stop.
Idiom rating (pass 2): ACCEPTABLE — second /review-rusty pass filed 1 new finding (.62) and confirms prior pass converged otherwise. The .62 finding is a real correctness bug (silent unwrap_or fallback on State token parse) that the prior pass mis-classified as canonical-first based on an incorrect cross-reference to mail-server:198; cross-walk against all 6 sibling crates shows filenode-server is the lone outlier. Every other workspace-coherent finding the prior pass deferred remains canonical-first and belongs in mail-server's review epic, not here.
Rust idiom confidence (pass 2): 25% chance of missed issue. Least confident flags: none from this pass (the single new finding .62 has a tight cross-walk to 6 sibling crates as the oracle, so its classification is strong). Convergence drivers: (1) ran 'cargo clippy --pedantic --nursery' for the first time — 73 warnings in filenode-server, of which 4 had engineering basis (too_many_lines on handle_filenode_copy, significant_drop_tightening on get_descendant_ids, redundant_closure_for_method_calls × 4 sites, cast_possible_truncation × 2) and ALL FOUR are workspace-canonical with byte-identical or larger-scale presence in canonical mail-server (13 too_many_lines hits, 89 redundant_closure hits across 8 files, many lock-holding sites); none should be filed here under the canonical-template propagation rule. (2) The sentinel-bool 'cascade_failed' pattern (filenode.rs:308, :739) the prior pass deferred is workspace-coherent — mail-server sieve.rs:834 has the same shape ('deactivation_failed'); not filing. (3) The four other ext-server siblings I cross-walked all do the right thing for since_state.parse, which gave a strong oracle for the .62 classification. Uncertainty driven by: (a) I did not run miri or loom against MemoryBackend to confirm the lock-tightening lint is observationally harmless given Arc<Mutex<Inner>> serialization (it is by inspection, but a concurrency test would raise confidence to >90%); (b) the JMAP-tco1 / JMAP-x2gd.84 workspace-canonical re-classification pattern suggests mail-server has its own /review-rusty epic where the cross-cutting canonical-first findings (too_many_lines, redundant_closure, mail-server's wrong since_state error variant) should land — I did not file there because the scope of this command is JMAP-510h, but a follow-up pass against mail-server's review epic (JMAP-q2wa or JMAP-j7pa) is warranted. Bikeshedding signal reached: stop.
